WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.252
Many iterator bugs appear when code modifies a container while traversing it.

2
00:00:04.252 --> 00:00:10.984
The code may erase an element and then increment an iterator that no longer refers to a valid position.

3
00:00:10.984 --> 00:00:14.528
It may insert into a vector and accidentally cause reallocation.

4
00:00:14.528 --> 00:00:20.197
It may remove the first or last element and forget that the loop boundary has changed.

5
00:00:20.197 --> 00:00:23.032
Safe mutation logic should make iterator validity visible.

6
00:00:23.032 --> 00:00:26.929
Erase operations often return an iterator to the next valid position.

7
00:00:26.929 --> 00:00:32.599
When the container contract provides that return value, use it instead of incrementing the invalidated iterator.

8
00:00:32.599 --> 00:00:38.977
Keep the loop structure simple enough that a reviewer can tell which iterator is valid after each branch.

9
00:00:38.977 --> 00:00:41.812
Sometimes the best solution is two-phase processing.

10
00:00:41.812 --> 00:00:46.064
First traverse the container and collect decisions, keys, indexes, or work items.

11
00:00:46.064 --> 00:00:48.544
Then apply modifications in a separate step.

12
00:00:48.544 --> 00:00:54.922
This can be clearer than mixing traversal, filtering, insertion, erasure, logging, and side effects inside one complicated loop.

13
00:00:54.922 --> 00:01:02.717
Tests should cover the cases where mutation code usually breaks: empty containers, one-element containers, removal of the first element, removal of

14
00:01:02.717 --> 00:01:07.678
the last element, repeated removal, no-op traversal, and insertion that forces capacity growth.

15
00:01:07.678 --> 00:01:09.450
Boundary tests are not busywork.

16
00:01:09.450 --> 00:01:11.930
They are how iterator assumptions become visible.

17
00:01:11.930 --> 00:01:17.599
In defensive C++ review, ask whether every mutation path leaves the next iterator state well defined.

18
00:01:17.599 --> 00:01:22.560
If the answer requires a long explanation, the code may need a clearer structure.
